are honors, so that's not helpful).\n\nWait, All Simples is a 2-point pattern where the hand has no terminal or honor tiles. The user has J3 and F1/F4, which are honors. So they can't have All Simples. So maybe not a consideration here.\n\nThe user's best option is to play a tile that's not part of any chow or pair. B7 is such a tile. Playing B7 would allow them to keep the two chows and the J3 pair. The other options like playing F1 or F4 would also be safe, but since the user has a pair of J3, maybe keeping the F1 and F4 is not necessary. So playing B7 is a good choice.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing B7 is optimal as it discards a non-vital tile, preserving key chows (W5-6-7 and T4-5-6) and the J3 pair. This maintains flexibility for forming additional chows or pungs while minimizing risk of breaking critical melds.\nReason: B7 is a middle tile not part of any existing meld, and its discard doesn't disrupt the two chows or the J3 pair. This preserves the potential for All Chows (2 points) or other patterns while reducing the number of tiles in hand for a more streamlined winning hand.\nAnswer: Play B7"}
