if they can get a B4 to form B2-B3-B4. \n\nBut since the user can't control the draws, they need to play safely. \n\nThe safest tile to discard is probably B2, as it's a single tile and not part of any critical meld. The user has a pair of B3s, so keeping them might help. \n\nAlternatively, discarding F1 or F2. \n\nBut the user's Seat Wind is East (F1), so keeping F1 could be beneficial for Seat Wind (2 points if they form a pung). \n\nThe user has one F1. If they discard F1, others might take it for a pung, but the user can't form a pung themselves. \n\nSimilarly, F2 is the prevailing wind. Discarding F2 might allow others to take it for a pung. \n\nGiven that, maybe discarding B2 is safer. \n\nSo the best move is to play B2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand contains two chows (T4-T5-T6 and W5-W6-W7) and a pair of B3s. Discarding B2, a single tile with 3 remaining in the wall, is safest as it doesn't disrupt critical chows or honor tile potential. B3 pair preservation supports future melding flexibility.\nReason: B2 is a single tile with no immediate chow/pung potential. Discarding it maintains concealed chows (T4-T5-T6, W5-W6-W7) and preserves B3 pair for possible Seven Pairs or additional melds. Other tiles like T4/5/6 or W5/6/7 risk breaking key chows.\nAnswer: Play B2"}
