wo), F1, J1, J3. \n\nBut in both cases, I need to form two more melds. \n\nAnother thought: The B3s could be a pair, and then I need two more melds. If I can form a Pung of B3, that would be good. But I need to get another B3. \n\nAlternatively, if I have a pair of J1 and J3, but that's two different tiles. \n\nWait, I have J1 and J3. If I can form a Pung of J1 or J3, but only one each. \n\nAlternatively, if I can form a pair of B3 and have another meld. \n\nLet me think about the current hand. After forming the T5-6-7 Chow, the remaining tiles are W3, W5, B3, B3, F1, J1, J3. That's 7 tiles. Need two melds and a pair. \n\nPossible melds: \n\n- W3 and W5 could be part of a Chow if I have W4. But I don't. \n\n- B3, B3, and another B3 would be a Pung. \n\n- F1, J1, J3 could be pairs. \n\nSo if I can get a third B3, that would be a Pung. Otherwise, maybe a pair of B3 and then another meld. \n\nBut without a third B3, I need another way. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a Pung of J1 or J3 if I can get another. \n\nBut given the current tiles, perhaps the best move is to play T4 to form the T5-6-7 Chow, then work on getting a third B3. \n\nLooking at the valid actions, playing T4 is an option. \n\nAnother valid action is playing B3. If I play B3, then maybe another player can take it. But since I have two B3s, playing one might not help me form a Pung unless I draw another. \n\nAlternatively, if I keep B3 and try to get another. \n\nBut given the unshown tiles, B3 has 4 left. If I play B3, there's a chance someone else might discard one, but I can't be sure. \n\nAnother angle: The current hand has T4-5-6-7. If I can form two Chows (T4-5-6 and T5-6-7), that would be two melds. But to do that, I need to discard T4 and T7. \n\nBut I can only discard one tile at a time. So first, play T4 to form T5-6-7. Then, in a later turn, play T7 to form T4-5-6. But that's two steps. However, in the current turn, I can only play one tile. \n\nSo the immediate action is to play T4. \n\n